This confirms the key semantics: `do_proc_dointvec` on a write parses values one at a time and writes each successfully parsed value into `*i` (table->data) immediately; on a parse error at a later element it breaks with `err != 0`, but earlier elements are already committed. So for `proc_do_sync_threshold` with `val[2]`: writing "abc" leaves both elements = old values (memcpy'd) → then the handler's own validation runs on old values (harmless, same values). Writing "5 abc" → val[0]=5 committed, val[1] stays old → rc=-EINVAL, handler re-validates partial val; validation may pass (e.g. old val[1]=20, new val[0]=5, 5<20 fine) → memcpy(valp, val) commits val[0]=5, discarding val[1]... wait val[1] = old value, so commit is a no-op for val[1] but val[0]=5 changed. The handler returns -EINVAL but committed the change. That's a return-vs-commit mismatch variant (semantic cousin: error-path commit), but not an uninitialized-stack use.

